# The line that matters most. seed.enc is your identity: 32 secret bytes,
# encrypted with a passphrase. A passphrase is guessable in a way that 32 random
# bytes are not, so treat the encryption as a delay, not a wall. And a file that
# has been in one commit is in the history for good - deleting it later removes
# it from the newest commit and from nothing else.
#
# If you ran "new" in this folder, this line is the only thing between that file
# and everyone who ever clones you.
seed.enc
*.enc

# did.json is public and completely safe to publish - it holds a DID and nothing
# secret. It is ignored anyway, because a repo that ships one person's did.json
# hands every reader a folder where the signer finds an identity it has no seed
# for, and the error that produces reads like a broken program. Put your DID in
# the README as text instead.
did.json

# Room exports and everything that travels with them. Megabytes of other
# people's messages, stale within the hour, and anyone can fetch their own.
# The .tcck.json sidecar is the one you might want to keep somewhere - it is what
# lets a later export be checked against an older checkpoint - but keep it with
# your records, not in a repo other people clone.
*.jsonl
*.meta.json
*.tcck.json
*.proof.json

# Nothing here needs installing, so this should stay empty forever. If it fills
# up, something you added has dependencies these six programs do not.
node_modules/
package-lock.json
